NOTE ON THE TESTS GATE — A FALSE RED, AND IT IS NOT W-020's

The first `verify W-020` run came back `tests: failed` and wrote that into the
front matter. A direct `npm test` on the same tree exits 0. Root cause, found
and reproduced:

  packages/pipeline/src/index.ts:75  runs each probatio command with
                                     filterEnv(process.env)
  packages/shim/src/redact.ts:34     drops any var whose NAME matches
                                     /TOKEN|SECRET|KEY|PASSWORD|CREDENTIAL/i

This sandbox exports GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=2 with GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0/VALUE_0 and
GIT_CONFIG_KEY_1/VALUE_1. filterEnv drops KEY_0 and KEY_1 (they match /KEY/i)
and keeps COUNT and both VALUEs. git then sees a config count of 2 with no
keys behind it and dies on every single invocation:

  error: missing config key GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0
  fatal: unable to parse command-line config

Confirmed directly:

  filterEnv(process.env): GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0 -> DROPPED, GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0 ->
  KEPT, GIT_CONFIG_KEY_1 -> DROPPED, GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_1 -> KEPT,
  GIT_CONFIG_COUNT -> KEPT

  env -u GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0 -u GIT_CONFIG_KEY_1 git ls-tree -r HEAD
  -> error: missing config key GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0

Re-running verify with the GIT_CONFIG_* block removed from the environment,
same commit, same tree hash 1f00fc18:

  tests: passed  ci/W-020-tests-1f00fc18.log
  lint:  passed  ci/W-020-lint-1f00fc18.log
  types: passed  ci/W-020-types-1f00fc18.log

So the tests gate is green on the merits, and the front matter now records the
honest result. This is a defect in filterEnv, not in W-020 — filterEnv owns no
part of this opus and W-020 touches neither file. A name-pattern env filter
that leaves a half-filtered multi-part variable set is a landmine for any
probatio command that shells out to git, which is most of them. It needs its
own opus: filter by variable GROUP, or drop GIT_CONFIG_COUNT whenever any
GIT_CONFIG_KEY_n is dropped. Filed as F1 below.


BLOCKING

B1 · `ready --spec` and `review --evidence` accept a path that escapes the
     officina, and write it into tracked front matter.

     packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:98   ready
     packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:284  review

     Both guard with `existsSync(join(root, path))` and nothing else. The
     brief states the requirement twice, in plain words: "Refuses when the
     spec file does not exist **under the officina**" and "`--evidence` must
     exist **under the officina**". `join()` is not containment. Demonstrated
     against a fixture officina:

       bisellium ready W-006 --spec ../../../../../etc/hostname --studio <off>
       -> W-006: spec=../../../../../etc/hostname state=building
       -> exit 0, and the front matter now reads
          spec: ../../../../../etc/hostname

       bisellium review W-004 --pass --evidence ../../../../../etc/hostname \
         --studio <off>
       -> W-004: review passed (../../../../../etc/hostname)
       -> exit 0, evidence recorded

     This is D-008's own discipline, blocking as of this cascade, and it is
     the recurrent class review×path-traversal-from-ids (L-004, L-011). The
     author knew the idiom — `safeRedsDir` at lifecycle.ts:353 does exactly
     the right thing for `red`'s opus id, by resolved-path relation, with a
     comment explaining why `startsWith` is not enough. Those two lines are
     the only place in packages/commands that resolves a caller-supplied path
     with a bare `join(root, ...)`; it is not inherited house style, it is new
     in this opus.

     The test file agrees with the gap. Behaviour 3's test (lifecycle.test.ts:
     151) covers "no brief exists"; behaviour 8's covers "evidence not found".
     Neither covers "resolves outside the officina", and the word `..` does
     not appear in the file. That is the tests×tests-that-agree-with-the-bug
     class (L-007, L-014).

     Fix: run both paths through the same resolved-path relation `safeRedsDir`
     already uses, and add the two red-then-green cases to behaviours 3 and 8.
     Small diff, and it closes two recurrent classes at once.


FINDINGS OF RECORD — not blocking, but they must be filed

F1 · filterEnv's partial env filter (see NOTE ON THE TESTS GATE). Not W-020's
     file. Needs an opus of its own; until it lands, every `bisellium verify`
     run in this sandbox reports a false tests-red and writes it into an
     opus's front matter. That is worse than a nuisance — it is a verifier
     that lies in the direction of failure, and the next reviewer will burn
     the same hour I did.

F2 · The red store is incomplete: behaviours 9, 10 and 11 have no log in
     studio/ci/reds/W-020/. Only 01-08 and 12 are there.

     The brief is explicit and repeats itself: behaviours 9-11 are `red`'s
     own, "run them, keep the failing output, and write them through
     `bisellium red` the moment it runs, before you implement
     ready/done/review". They were captured — honestly, before the file
     existed, as an ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND — but they were left in the interim
     store at ~/.bisellium-evidence/cascade-6/w020/behaviours-09-11-red.log,
     as one file covering three behaviours. The evidence exists and is real;
     it is in the wrong place, and the place it is in is one W-021's rule can
     never read. I am not treating unrecoverable evidence as a gate: the code
     is green now and `red` will rightly refuse to record a passing command,
     so these three cannot be re-captured. It goes on the record instead.

F3 · The check rule that should have caught F2 cannot. `checkRedEvidence`
     (packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.ts:147) only fires for opera in
     building/verifying/review. W-020 was moved to `done` in the same session
     the reds were written, so `opus.red_evidence` skipped it and has never
     once looked at this opus. A blocking evidence rule that stops looking the
     moment an opus closes is a rule that can be outrun by closing fast. That
     is W-021's rule, not W-020's code, but it is why F2 reached me unflagged
     and it belongs in the retro.

F4 · The nine reds that are present are one red, recorded nine times.
     01-08 and 12 are byte-identical apart from the `# behaviour:` line —
     same command, same tree dirty:c629ec44, same output, all written within
     the same minute:

       SyntaxError: The requested module './lifecycle.js' does not provide an
       export named 'runDone'

     That is a genuine red and it was genuinely produced, not backfilled —
     `red` cannot be made to record a passing command and I confirmed that
     myself. But a module that fails to resolve an export proves that the file
     does not load; it does not show behaviour 5's assertion failing, because
     no assertion in the file ever ran. The engineering lex §7 asks for "the
     recorded red for every behaviour its spec listed". Nine copies of one
     load error is one red wearing nine hats. Recurrent class
     review×evidence-backfilled-or-absent (L-001, L-008, L-017 — this is the
     fourth cascade).

F5 · `review`'s return edge is dead in practice, so L-016's wall is only half
     down. A `--fail` sends an opus back to `building` only from state
     `review`. The brief itself concedes that "nothing in this codebase moves
     an opus into `verifying` or `review`". So the one state the edge fires
     from is a state nothing can produce, and a failed review on an opus in
     `building` or `done` leaves it exactly where it was, with no command able
     to move it: `ready` refuses anything that is not greenlit/halted, and
     `done` refuses anything already done. Recording my own FAIL below
     demonstrates this: W-020 will sit in `done` carrying a failed review
     gate, and no verb in this CLI can return it to `building` for the fix.
     That is not a reason to withhold the fail — it is the reason the next
     opus needs a way back.

F6 · `red` never checks that the opus exists. Every other verb refuses an
     unknown opus with exit 2; `red` cheerfully creates the directory:

       bisellium red W-999-nope --behaviour 1 --studio <off> -- false
       -> W-999-nope: red recorded for behaviour 1 -> ci/reds/W-999-nope/01.log
       -> exit 0

     The Interfaces section lists "unknown opus" as an exit-2 case for these
     commands. Containment is correct (that part is well done), so this is
     litter rather than a hole, but the store is W-021's input and stray
     directories in it are noise.

F7 · `done` leaves `traditio.stage` behind. Every opus this cascade closed
     carries `traditio.stage: building` against `state: done`, and check
     raises `traditio.stage` advisories for W-016 through W-021. `done`
     reproduces the gap in the `scripts/opus-close.ts` it deleted. Advisory
     only, but it is six advisories that will never clear on their own.

F8 · `done`'s wrong-state refusal exits 2 where the brief's exit-code contract
     reads 1 ("a refusal the recorded state does not support"). Behaviour 5
     only pins the gate refusals at 1 and behaviour 2 pins ready's state
     refusal at 2, so this is under-specified rather than wrong. Worth one
     line in the contract next time.

F9 · Both commits end `Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5`. CLAUDE.md asks for a
     trailer "naming the sella that wrote it" — that is `builder-a`, not a
     model name. Cosmetic, and it is the whole repo's habit, not this opus's
     invention.


WHAT IS GOOD — and most of this opus is good

  Files-owned discipline is clean, and I checked it line by line rather than
  taking the commit message's word for it. `packages/commands/src/writes.ts`
  came out export-only: nine `export` keywords added and not one other
  character changed, the five existing write commands byte-identical.
  `packages/cli/src/main.ts` is four dispatch lines and four USAGE lines
  across the two commits, `FLAGS_BY_COMMAND` untouched, dispatch placed ahead
  of the generic parser exactly as the brief required. Nothing outside the
  owned list is touched in either commit. After cascade 4b this is the thing
  I most expected to find broken and it is not.

  `red` is the piece that had to be right, and it is. I drove it end to end:

    - non-zero command: writes ci/reds/<opus>/03.log with the six headers in
      the decreed order, `# exit: 7` equal to the command's real exit code,
      `# at` equal to `--now`, `# tree:` a real certificate, and stdout and
      stderr interleaved verbatim after one blank line.
    - exit-0 refusal: "red: command exited 0 — that command passed, nothing
      recorded", exit 1, and I confirmed no file AND no ci/reds/ directory was
      created. The refusal that makes the store worth having works.
    - --behaviour 0, -1, 1.5, a missing `--`, an empty command, and opus ids
      `a/b`, `../evil` and `..` all refuse with exit 2 and write nothing.
    - a second red for the same behaviour overwrites that behaviour's log and
      leaves its neighbour alone.

  The L-016 wall is genuinely down, which is the reason this opus exists. I
  recorded a pass on a fixture opus, then a fail over it, and watched the
  evidence path get overwritten and both rounds ride `workflow.gate_evaluated`
  with `workflow.review.round` 1 and 2. A `--fail` on an opus in state
  `review` returned it to `building`; on an opus in `building` it left the
  state alone. No write touched timeline/patron.jsonl, as decreed.

  `ready` and `done` honour their guards. `ready` on greenlit set `state`,
  `spec:` and one `workflow.state_changed`, and I diffed the body byte for
  byte — identical. With no `spec` probatio in the manifest it correctly
  recorded no spec gate. `done` refused W-004 with exit 1 and "gates not
  passed: patron" because patron is recorded `pending` — the recorded-human-
  gate asymmetry the brief asked for, implemented off `readFront` and not off
  a regex, which is precisely what got `scripts/opus-close.ts` deleted.

  All four verbs print their own usage and exit 2 on no arguments. Cascade
  4b's ship-a-command-nothing-reaches failure does not recur.

  58 assertions over real temp-studio fixtures with byte-identity checks. The
  suite is good work. Its one blind spot is B1's.


DISPOSITION

B1 is a two-line fix in a file this opus owns, plus two test cases. Fix it,
capture the reds for those two cases before the fix, and bring it back for
round 3. F2, F3 and F4 go to the retro as a fourth recurrence of
review×evidence-backfilled-or-absent, with F3 as the reason it was not caught:
close the rule's blind spot on `done` opera, or the store can always be
outrun. F1 needs its own opus before the next cascade, or the next reviewer
inherits a verifier that lies.

Recorded with `bisellium review W-020 --fail --evidence ci/W-020-review-2.log
--round 2 --sella eng-lead` — this opus's own command, which is the only
reason this verdict can be written down at all.
